<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>One vote, time and change — evidence summary</title><style>body{font:16px/1.65 system-ui,sans-serif;color:#27352e;background:#f5f4ed;max-width:820px;padding:30px;margin:auto}h1{font:42px/1.15 Georgia}h2{font-size:22px;margin-top:30px}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px}td,th{text-align:left;padding:9px;border-bottom:1px solid #cdd5c8}.note{background:#e5ecdf;padding:20px}small{color:#586751}a{color:#37584b}@media print{body{background:white;padding:0;font-size:11pt}h2{break-after:avoid}tr{break-inside:avoid}}</style></head><body><small>SOUTH AFRICAN ELECTORAL IMPACT LAB · EVIDENCE SUMMARY</small><h1>One vote. Years of waiting. Change you may not want.</h1><p>The question: can a tiny individual input produce a change you value, within a meaningful part of your life?</p><h2>Three claims, three verdicts</h2><p class="note"><strong>Individual seat impact:</strong> {{checks}} national one-ballot scenarios; {{changes}} changed seat allocations. The narrow historical claim is supported within the tested model.<br><strong>A decade or more of waiting:</strong> not established as a general rule. Observed changes depend on the starting point and the meaning of “change”.<br><strong>A result worth your time:</strong> unanswered. Vote totals do not measure your preferences, wellbeing or improvements in services.</p><h2>How the system connects</h2><p>Ballots → 400 National Assembly seats → political decisions → implementation → effects on your life. The Assembly elects the President, passes laws and oversees the executive. A majority means at least 201 seats. Becoming the largest party, losing a majority and improving lives are different outcomes. Only representation is measured here.</p><h2>Regional → national scale (2019)</h2><p>National means countrywide. The nine regions and overseas ballots contribute to {{ballots2019}} valid national ballots. At one ballot per second, this is about 202 days of nonstop counting. Your ballot occupies one second in that analogy; this is not the actual counting process.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Where cast</th><th>National ballots</th><th>Regional NA seats</th></tr></thead><tbody>{{#each regions}}<tr><td>{{name}}</td><td>{{votes}}</td><td>{{seats}}</td></tr>{{/each}}</tbody></table><p>200 regional seats plus 200 compensatory seats make 400 Assembly seats. Regional Assembly seats are not provincial-legislature seats. In 2024, separate national and regional ballots both contributed to the Assembly calculation; do not merge their counts with the 2019 geographic illustration.</p><h2>What happened over time?</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Election</th><th>Largest party's seats / 400</th></tr></thead><tbody>{{#each timeline}}<tr><td>{{year}}</td><td>{{seats}}</td></tr>{{/each}}</tbody></table><p><strong>30 years:</strong> 1994 to the first result in this series without a single-party majority, in 2024. <strong>20 years:</strong> 2004 peak to 2024. <strong>5 years:</strong> 2019's 230 seats to 2024's 159, crossing the 201-seat threshold. The largest-party series tracks the ANC throughout; this measures concentration, not whether a party's result is desirable.</p><p>These are seven election observations, not thirty annual observations. An annualised difference is the change between endpoints divided by years elapsed. It does not show steady yearly change or predict a future waiting time. Any endpoint after 2024 is unobserved here.</p><h2>How the evidence was formed</h2><p>IEC aggregate reports → preserved files with URLs, retrieval dates and SHA-256 fingerprints → extracted counts reconciled with published totals → seat calculations checked against all 211 national party-election results → hypothetical one-ballot changes and recalculation. The scanned 1999 seat table required checked manual transcription. File fingerprints establish which bytes were used, not an independent audit of ballot counting.</p><p>Another {{regionalChecks}} eligible regional one-ballot scenarios across 2004–2024 produced no regional seat changes. These scenarios are not independent elections and do not estimate the probability that a future ballot matters.</p><h2>Limits that affect the conclusion</h2><p>Complete minimum-seat and minimum-majority searches across all parties are unfinished. Candidate-list exhaustion, regional overhang redistribution and winning-independent counterfactuals are not implemented in the national model. The 2024 national tests hold regional votes fixed. Municipal elections and socioeconomic outcomes have not been ingested.</p><p class="note"><strong>Conclusion:</strong> small observed individual seat influence; uneven collective electoral change; no guaranteed personal benefit or waiting time. These data do not decide whether participation is worthwhile for you.</p><p><a href="{{sourceUrl}}">Original IEC 2019 report</a> · <a href="https://www.gov.za/about-government/national-assembly-na">Assembly functions</a> · <a href="https://www.elections.org.za/pw/elections/whats-new-in-the-2024-elections-electoral-amendment-act">IEC 2024 system</a></p><p><a href="/">Interactive explanation</a> · <a href="/data/report.json">Calculation outputs and source registry</a></p></body></html>
